<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nivkh people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nivkh, Gilyak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nivkh are an Indigenous people of the lower Amur river and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakhalin">Sakhalin</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-far-east">Russian Far East</a>, numbering a few thousand. They traditionally lived by fishing, sea-mammal hunting and dog-sled travel, and their language is an isolate unrelated to its neighbours. Those in southern Sakhalin came under Japanese rule in Karafuto from 1905 to 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nivkh-people.jpg" alt="Nivkh people" width="320" height="154" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nivkh_People.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nivkh call themselves by a word meaning the people; Russian explorers of the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries termed them Gilyak, a name linguists dispute and that Soviet officials replaced with the Nivkh endonym. They are believed to be the original inhabitants of the region and to descend from a proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> people that migrated from the Transbaikal region during the Late Pleistocene. Their origins are hard to discern from current archaeological research. Their fishing and coastal sea-mammal hunting resemble those of the Koryak and Itelmen on the Kamchatka Peninsula, and their dog-sledge rigging is similar too.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Vassili Poyarkov was the first Russian to write of the Nivkh, in 1643. For many centuries they were tributaries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nerchinsk">Treaty of Nerchinsk</a> in 1689 they acted as intermediaries between Russians, Manchus and Japanese, and also the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ainu">Ainu</a>, who were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Japanese. Contact with the southern Sakhalin Ainu was generally hostile, though the two traded. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> annexed the region after two treaties in 1858 and 1860.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> the Soviet government embraced the name Nivkh as a mark of native self-determination, created a brief autonomous okrug and granted extensive fishing rights, which lasted until the 1960s. Other policies proved devastating: the Nivkh were forced into kolkhoz collectives, and fishermen who held that ploughing the earth was a sin were hard to convert to agriculture. In the 1930s Nivkh people often disappeared under suspicion of pro-Japanese sentiment. When the collectives were abandoned after 1991, rapid economic hardship followed for a people that depended on them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Nivkh were traditionally fishermen, hunters and dog breeders, living near the coast in summer and wintering inland along streams to catch salmon. Villages held three or four households shared by several families, drawn from two to eight clans. A clan, khal, was united by marriage ties and a common derived deity, and arranged marriages and settled disputes. Winter dwellings were a round dugout called the ryv or a gabled one-room house heated by a kang.</p><p>Traditional religion was animist and worked through shamans, who diagnosed and cured disease. The Bear Festival, held in January or February, centred on a bear raised for several years in a corral by local women, who treated it like a child. The bear was held to be a sacred manifestation of ancestors and gods, and was dressed, offered a banquet and sacrificed. The Nivkh language is an isolate or small family. Most Nivkh now speak Russian, and under five percent speak Nivkh; the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,842.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nivkh people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On Sakhalin they fought the <b>Nivkh</b>, and after the Mongols set up a post at Nurgan on the Amur in 1263, the Mongol-ruled Yuan dynasty attacked them in 1264; the northern Sakhalin Ainu resisted until 1308, then paid tribute.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ainu">Ainu · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Ainu, <b>Nivkh</b> and Uilta communities were pressed to assimilate.</blockquote><a href="/state/karafuto-prefecture">Karafuto Prefecture</a></li></ul></section>
